# 1. Distributed web infrastructure

Diagram: https://github.com/luisfe05/holbertonschool-system_engineering-devops/blob/main/web_infrastructure_design/1-distributed_web_infrastructure.png

## What happens when a user visits www.foobar.com

DNS resolves `www.foobar.com` to the IP of the load balancer. The HAProxy load
balancer receives the request and forwards it to one of the two servers. On
that server, Nginx handles the request, the application server runs the code
and queries MySQL, and the response goes back through the load balancer to the
user.

## Infrastructure

- 1 load balancer (HAProxy).
- 2 servers, each with: 1 web server (Nginx), 1 application server, 1 set of
  application files (code base) and 1 MySQL database.
- MySQL is configured as a Primary-Replica cluster: Server 1 holds the Primary
  and Server 2 holds the Replica.

## Specifics

- **Why each additional element is added:**
  - *Load balancer (HAProxy):* spreads the traffic between the servers so no
    single server is overloaded, and lets the site keep working if one server
    goes down.
  - *Second server:* adds redundancy and capacity. If one server fails or is
    being updated, the other keeps serving users.
  - *Database replica:* keeps a live copy of the data (backup/failover) and
    can answer read queries to take load off the Primary.
- **Distribution algorithm:** **Round Robin**. The load balancer sends the
  first request to server 1, the second to server 2, the third to server 1
  again, and so on, in rotation, so each server receives an equal share of
  requests.
- **Active-Active or Active-Passive:** this is an **Active-Active** setup,
  because both servers receive traffic at the same time.
  - *Active-Active:* all nodes are working and serving requests simultaneously.
    Better resource use and capacity, and if one node fails the others absorb
    its load.
  - *Active-Passive:* only one node serves traffic; the other waits on standby
    and takes over only when the active one fails. Simpler, but the standby
    capacity is idle.
- **How a Primary-Replica (Master-Slave) cluster works:** all writes
  (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) go to the Primary node. The Primary records the
  changes in its binary log and the Replica copies and applies them, so it
  holds the same data. The Replica can serve reads.
- **Primary vs Replica regarding the application:** the Primary accepts reads
  and writes, so the application must send every write to it. The Replica is
  read-only for the application: it can serve read queries (SELECT) but writes
  must not be sent to it. If the Primary fails, a Replica can be promoted.

## Issues with this infrastructure

- **SPOF:**
  - The load balancer: if it fails, nobody can reach the site.
  - The Primary MySQL: if it fails, the application cannot write (until a
    Replica is promoted).
- **Security issues:** no firewall (any port is reachable), and no HTTPS (the
  traffic is not encrypted, so it can be read or modified in transit).
- **No monitoring:** nothing watches the servers, so failures or overload are
  detected only when users complain.
